
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">Free Narcan &amp; training</p>
      <h1>Anyone can learn to reverse an overdose.</h1>
      <p>Free kits, free training, no questions asked — in Flagler County since 2019.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="hero__grid">
        <div>
          <h2>What we do</h2>
          <p>
            Narcan (naloxone) is a nasal spray that reverses an opioid overdose. It is safe, it
            is simple, and it does nothing at all if the person turns out not to be overdosing.
            Using it takes about as long as reading this paragraph.
          </p>
          <p>
            In 2019 the Foundation became the <strong>first local nonprofit to secure a grant
            from the Florida Department of Children and Families</strong> to provide Narcan at
            no cost to people who need it. We have been handing out kits ever since.
          </p>

          <div class="callout callout--gold">
            <p class="mb-0" style="font-size:1.35rem;font-weight:600;line-height:1.35">
              “If they’re dead, they can’t recover.”
            </p>
            <p class="mb-0 mt-5">
              That is the whole argument, and it is why this program grew past the drug court.
              Everything else the Foundation does depends on the person still being here.
            </p>
          </div>

          <h3 class="mt-6">Who we have trained and supplied</h3>
          <ul class="list-check">
            <li><strong>Every school in the Flagler County school district</strong> — we provided
            the Narcan and the training</li>
            <li><strong>The Flagler County Tax Collector’s office</strong> — over 100,000 people a
            year come through those doors for taxes and vehicle licensing. They came to us and
            asked.</li>
            <li><strong>The Flagler County Health Department</strong> — our team trained theirs</li>
            <li>Nursing homes, businesses, churches, civic clubs, families, and anyone who asks</li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            That is the shift this program caused. We started out supporting people inside the
            drug court program, and we ended up as one of the county’s Narcan educators.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="hero__media">
          <picture>
            <source srcset="assets/img/narcan-outreach.webp" type="image/webp">
            <img src="assets/img/narcan-outreach.jpg"
                 alt="The Foundation's outreach table at a community event, with free Narcan kits and information sheets laid out under a canopy beside a Road to Recovery banner."
                 loading="lazy" width="1200" height="1200">
          </picture>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--tint">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section__head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Upcoming</p>
        <h2>Next training class</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="grid grid--2">
        <div class="callout callout--gold">
          <h3>[NEXT CLASS DATE]</h3>
          <div class="placeholder">
            <p><strong>[CLIENT MUST SUPPLY]</strong> We need:</p>
            <ul>
              <li>Date and start time</li>
              <li>Venue name and street address</li>
              <li>Roughly how long the session runs</li>
              <li>Whether registration is required or people can walk in</li>
            </ul>
            <p class="mb-0">If classes run on a regular schedule (say, first Friday monthly),
            tell us that instead and we will publish the pattern so this never goes stale.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="card">
          <h3>Request a training</h3>
          <p>
            If your business, church, civic club, school or neighborhood group would like a
            presentation and training, we will come to you and run a session. There is no charge,
            and participants leave with a kit.
          </p>
          <p>
            Sessions run about twenty minutes for the basics — recognizing an overdose,
            administering the spray, calling 911, and staying until help arrives.
          </p>
          <a class="btn btn--primary" href="contact.html?subject=narcan">Request a training</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--warm">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section__head">
        <p class="eyebrow">“I don’t need that”</p>
        <h2>People tell us that at every event. We are glad they don’t.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          Then we ask the second question: do you know somebody who might? And if you came across
          a stranger face down on the ground, would you know what to do? Carrying Narcan is like
          knowing CPR. You are not carrying it for yourself.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="grid grid--3">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Unintentional overdose is at a record high</h3>
          <p class="mb-0">
            Most of it is fentanyl, and most of the people it kills had no idea they had taken
            any. Somebody picks up a pill that looks like something familiar, and that is the end
            of it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="card">
          <h3>Older adults get confused on their medication</h3>
          <p class="mb-0">
            A doubled dose of a legitimate prescription can stop someone breathing just as surely
            as anything bought on a street corner. Narcan does not care how the opioid got there.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="card">
          <h3>A kit we gave away saved a woman in her eighties</h3>
          <p class="mb-0">
            A Flagler County nursing home had one of our kits on the shelf. A resident on a
            legitimate fentanyl patch overdosed on it — her body simply could not handle the dose.
            Staff used the kit, she survived, and her medication was adjusted.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section__head">
        <p class="eyebrow">If you are worried about someone</p>
        <h2>What to do in an overdose</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          This is general public guidance, not medical advice. If you are in doubt, call 911.
        </p>
      </div>

      <ol class="steps">
        <li>
          <h3>Give Narcan</h3>
          <p class="mb-0">Do this first. Spray one dose into one nostril. If there is no response
          after two to three minutes, give a second dose in the other nostril.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Call 911</h3>
          <p class="mb-0">Call as soon as the first dose is in. Florida's Good Samaritan law
          protects people who call for help during an overdose from certain drug-possession
          charges — calling is the right move even if there are substances present.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Keep them breathing</h3>
          <p class="mb-0">Follow the dispatcher's instructions. If the person is breathing on
          their own but unresponsive, lay them on their side so they cannot choke.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Stay until help arrives</h3>
          <p class="mb-0">Narcan wears off before many opioids do, so an overdose can return.
          The person needs to be seen by paramedics regardless of how well they seem to recover.</p>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <div class="callout mt-6 narrow">
        <h3>Where to get a free kit</h3>
        <p class="mb-0">
          Find us at community events around Flagler County, or
          <a href="contact.html?subject=narcan">get in touch</a> and we will arrange one. Kits
          are free, and we do not ask why you want one.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--dark">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="hero__grid">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Fund the kits</p>
          <h2>Every kit we give away was paid for by someone local.</h2>
          <p>
            Grants come and go, and we do not have one running right now. Community giving is what
            keeps a supply on the table between grant cycles — which is exactly when people tend
            to need one.
          </p>
          <p>
            Over the counter, a two-dose kit costs $45 to $50. When we started this in 2019 it was
            over $200, before the FDA moved naloxone over the counter. It has never cost the
            person receiving it anything, and it never will.
          </p>
          <div class="btn-row mt-5">
            <a class="btn btn--donate btn--lg" href="ways-to-give.html" data-donate>Fund Narcan kits</a>
            <a class="btn btn--outline" href="ways-to-give.html#monthly">Give monthly</a>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="stats">
            <div class="stat">
              <p class="stat__figure">$45</p>
              <p class="stat__label">puts one two-dose Narcan kit in someone’s hands, free</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
